---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/robotics/foundational/configuration-space">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Robotics' most powerful single abstraction is also its least physical: replace the whole
    robot with one point. A robot arm's "position" is not one number but a list — every joint
    angle at once. The <strong>configuration space</strong> (C-space) takes that list and
    treats it as the coordinates of a single imaginary point: a six-jointed arm becomes one
    point drifting through a six-dimensional space, where each dimension is one joint's
    angle. Nothing about the robot has changed; everything about the <em>problems</em> has.
    "Where is the robot?" is now "where is the point?", and the fiendishly tangled question
    "can the arm reach around the pillar without hitting it?" becomes a clean geometric one:
    can a path be drawn between two points that avoids certain forbidden regions?
  </p>
  <p>
    The value of the move is uniformity. Robots come in absurd variety — grippers, wheels,
    snake spines, humanoids — but every one of them has a configuration, and every
    configuration is a point in some space. Once the whole field agrees to phrase its
    problems inside C-space, one set of tools serves them all: the same mathematics that
    plans a two-joint gripper plans a thirty-joint walking machine, just in a bigger space.
    The dimension gets punishing, and much of the field's algorithmic ingenuity exists to
    survive it.
  </p>
  <p>
    This chapter covers the three ideas the abstraction stands on: how to count a robot's
    degrees of freedom honestly (Grübler's formula), how the task and the workspace relate
    to the space of configurations, and how constraints — especially the sneaky
    nonholonomic kind that make cars unable to slide sideways — shape what paths through
    that space are even possible.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from robot to point: the C-space move" widget="equationStep" config={dofCfg} />

  <h2>Degrees of freedom and Grübler's formula</h2>
  <p>
    The number of <em>degrees of freedom</em> (DOF) is the number of independent numbers
    needed to pin down the robot's configuration — the dimension of its C-space. A free
    rigid body in the plane needs three (two positions, one rotation); in space it needs
    six. Joints spend those freedoms: a revolute joint (a hinge) contributes one, a ball-
    and-socket joint three. So a robot arm with three hinged joints in the plane has
    DOF = 3 — three numbers describe it completely.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Grübler's formula</em> turns this into honest bookkeeping for linkages, where joints
    form loops that constrain each other: in the plane, DOF = 3 × (links − 1) − 2 × (one-
    freedom joints). Take the four-bar linkage — the mechanism inside your windshield wiper
    and a thousand engines: four links (including the ground), four revolute joints, one
    loop. DOF = 3 × (4 − 1) − 2 × 4 = 9 − 8 = 1. One input — spin the crank — and the whole
    mechanism's motion is determined. That single number is the design's certificate: the
    linkage will move predictably, never flop and never jam. Get the count wrong in either
    direction and no quality of motor rescues you: a mechanism with fewer degrees of freedom
    than actuators binds and fights itself; one with more flops uncontrolled.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more example cements the method, because it shows joints of different kinds sharing
    a formula. The slider–crank — the linkage that turns every car engine's piston into
    rotation — has four links (crank, connecting rod, piston, ground), three revolute joints
    and one prismatic (sliding) joint, all worth one freedom each. Grübler: DOF = 3 ×
    (4 − 1) − 2 × 4 = 1. Again exactly one: drive the crank and the piston must follow its
    prescribed dance, which is precisely what an engine requires — one input, one
    coordinated output. The formula's power is that it delivers this verdict from a parts
    list, before anything is built or simulated.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same counting explains a puzzling industry fact: industrial arms commonly have six
    joints, not five or seven. Six is the minimum that can place the gripper anywhere in
    space with any orientation (a free body's 6 DOF). Seven appears on the most capable
    arms — elephant trunks and human arms are redundancy of the same kind — giving the
    controller slack to reach around obstacles while holding the gripper pose fixed. In
    C-space terms: a 7-dimensional space, with a 6-dimensional "task surface" carved out
    inside it, and infinitely many points of the space projecting onto each point of the
    surface. That infinity is redundancy, and it is a feature.
  </p>

  <h2>Task and workspace</h2>
  <p>
    Three spaces matter, and keeping them distinct prevents half of all beginner confusion.
    The <em>configuration space</em> is the space of the robot's joint values. The
    <em>workspace</em> is the set of points the end-effector can physically reach — a
    property of the arm's geometry. The <em>task space</em> is whatever the job actually
    lives in: two dimensions for drawing on paper, six for spray-painting a car body, five
    or fewer for most assembly operations once alignment is handled by fixtures.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two-link planar arm makes the workspace concrete. With links of one metre each, the
    gripper can reach any point within two metres of the shoulder — but only if the arm can
    fully extend, and it can also fold the gripper all the way back to the shoulder itself.
    The reachable set is therefore not a disk but an <em>annulus</em>: radii from 0 to 2 m.
    The geometry hides a classic computational wrinkle: most reachable points correspond to
    <em>two</em> configurations — "elbow up" and "elbow down" — so the mapping from task to
    configuration (inverse kinematics) is not one-to-one, and the planner must choose. The
    forward direction (configuration to position) is easy trigonometry; the inverse is where
    the mathematics gets interesting, and it is the inverse a robot actually needs.
  </p>
  <p>
    The spaces also differ in the direction of difficulty. Workspaces are small — at most
    six dimensions, usually fewer — while configuration spaces grow with every joint, and
    planning happens in the bigger one. A task as humble as "wipe this tabletop" may demand
    navigating a 6-dimensional C-space to keep the wrist legal while the hand traces a flat
    2-dimensional path. The task never sees the difficulty; the configuration space carries
    it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Real arms also carry <em>joint limits</em>, which fence the C-space even in empty
    rooms: a hinge that cannot pass 180 degrees turns its angle line into a segment. Add
    gravity and the picture sharpens again — every point in C-space implies a torque load
    on every joint, so two configurations reaching the same task point can differ wildly in
    energy cost and payload capacity. This is why the C-space picture, fences and terrain
    included, is the planner's true map rather than a mathematician's conceit: the point
    that wanders into the wrong region breaks a robot, and the point that lingers on steep
    terrain burns a motor.
  </p>

  <h2>Holonomic and nonholonomic constraints</h2>
  <p>
    A <em>holonomic</em> constraint is a restriction on configurations themselves — the
    classic is a door hinge: the door's angle is fixed to the wall's, full stop. Holonomic
    constraints shrink the C-space (the hinge's two links are really one DOF), and the
    planner can simply plan in the smaller space.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Nonholonomic</em> constraints are the subtle kind: restrictions on <em>velocity</em>
    that do not reduce the set of reachable positions. The canonical example is a car. Its
    configuration needs three numbers in the plane — position x, y and heading θ — so its
    C-space is 3-dimensional. But the driver controls only two things: steering angle and
    forward speed. Rolling without slipping forbids sideways motion: the car's velocity must
    point along its heading, always. There is no configuration the car cannot eventually
    reach — yet it cannot move sideways <em>one centimetre</em>, ever.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> nonholonomic constraints reduce the robot's degrees of
    freedom. They do not — the car's C-space stays 3-dimensional, and every point in it is
    reachable. What they constrain is the <em>directions of available motion at each point</em>,
    and therefore the shape of feasible paths. Parallel parking is the everyday proof: the
    gap is trivially reachable in C-space, but only through a curved, back-and-forth
    manoeuvre, because the straight path would require the forbidden sideways slide. The
    distinction matters enormously in planning: a holonomic robot can plan point-to-point
    and follow any path; a car-like robot must plan paths that respect its turning radius,
    which is a genuinely harder computational problem.
  </p>

  <h2>Obstacles move into the space too</h2>
  <p>
    The C-space's cleverest trick is what it does to collisions. Checking whether a shaped,
    articulated robot intersects a shaped world, joint by joint, is a geometry nightmare —
    and you would have to redo it for every instant of every candidate path. C-space
    replaces the nightmare with a map: compute, once, the set of <em>configurations</em> in
    which the robot would collide with anything, and mark that whole set as forbidden
    terrain. The living room's sofa becomes a curved region in joint-angle space; the
    question "can the arm reach the shelf?" becomes "can a path dodge that region?" — the
    same question a GPS answers, in stranger coordinates. Computing the forbidden region
    exactly is hard for high dimensions, which is one more reason planners end up sampling
    and testing points rather than mapping the terrain in full.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    C-spaces have topology, and it bites. A single revolute joint's angle lives on a circle:
    359 degrees is adjacent to 0, not to 720. A two-joint arm therefore lives not on a
    rectangle but on a torus — a doughnut, where "past the edge" wraps around. Planners that
    ignore the wrap-around plan across a seam that does not exist, producing spurious
    "collisions" at the 0/360 boundary. The torus is the friendliest case; closed-chain
    mechanisms have C-spaces whose very dimension can change from place to place, and whose
    geometry researchers still characterise case by case.
  </p>
  <p>
    Obstacles in the world grow when mapped into C-space — the technical device is the
    Minkowski sum: a round robot sweeping past a corner forbids a larger, corner-round
    region, every configuration where any part of the robot would touch. And dimension
    remains the enemy, quantifiably. Discretise each joint at a modest 63 values (0.1 radian
    steps over a typical range): a 2-DOF space is 3,969 cells — trivial. Six DOF is about
    6.25 × 10¹⁰ — dead on arrival. A humanoid's 30-plus DOF makes storing the grid, never
    mind searching it, physically absurd. This is precisely why sampling-based planners —
    the RRT family and probabilistic roadmaps — took over the field: they probe the space
    with random samples, connect near neighbours, and grow trees or roadmaps sparsely,
    never enumerating. They surrender guaranteed optimality and even guaranteed success,
    keeping only <em>probabilistic completeness</em> — given a solution exists, the
    probability of finding one approaches 1 as sampling continues. That trade — certainty
    about completeness for practicality about dimension — is the defining bargain of modern
    motion planning, and the open questions run from planning under uncertainty and contact
    (where C-space itself becomes probabilistic) to whole-body planning for humanoids, where
    dynamics, balance and nonholonomic foot-placement constraints all bind at once.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Lynch &amp; Park, <em>Modern Robotics</em>, Ch. 2 (official companion site); LaValle, <em>Planning Algorithms</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
